<h1>Credential Verification</h1>
<p class="subtitle">An optional provider request can add evidence. It can also transmit a credential and misclassify it.</p>

<h2 id="opt-in">Off by default</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan --all --verify</span>
<span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate verify --all</span></div>
<p>The default is <code>verify: false</code>. A generated hook does not put <code>--verify</code> on its command line, but it exports <code>GATE_HOOK_TYPE</code> while the CLI checks <code>GATE_PRE_COMMIT</code>. Consequently, a project or user config with <code>verify: true</code> can still trigger verification from that hook. <code>--no-verify</code> disables it for a run, and <code>GATE_OFFLINE=1</code> skips it. Gate also skips verification when <code>NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=0</code> is present.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Hook safety setting:</strong> leave <code>verify</code> false in both user and project configuration for hook-driven scans until the environment-variable mismatch is fixed. If you need verification, invoke an explicit reviewed command outside the commit hook.</div>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Verification sends the matched credential value over the network.</strong> Most adapters put it in an authorization header; GCP puts it in an <code>access_token</code> query parameter; Supabase sends it to a project-specific <code>*.supabase.co</code> host derived from the token. Provider infrastructure, DNS, proxies, endpoint logs, and your network can observe request metadata, and URL logging can expose query parameters.</div>

<h2 id="outcomes">What outcomes do</h2>
<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Current effect</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>live</code></td><td>Severity is upgraded to critical.</td><td>The adapter's endpoint accepted the credential for that particular request.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>inactive</code></td><td>Severity is downgraded to low and marked auto-downgraded.</td><td>The adapter interpreted the response as rejected. This is not proof that the credential is harmless.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>unknown</code></td><td>Severity is unchanged.</td><td>No adapter, insufficient material, or an unrecognized response.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>timeout</code> / <code>error</code></td><td>Severity is unchanged.</td><td>The check did not establish liveness.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>skipped</code></td><td>Severity is unchanged.</td><td>The start-time budget check was exceeded.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Do not treat “inactive” as safe.</strong> Missing scopes, changed APIs, account state, credential shape, rate limits, or adapter mistakes can look like rejection. Because Gate currently lowers inactive findings to <code>low</code>, an action using the default <code>fail-on: high</code> may stop blocking that finding. Rotate exposed credentials regardless of this label.</div>

<h2 id="adapters">Current adapter table</h2>
<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Rule IDs</th><th>Request</th><th>Important limit</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>github-pat, github-oauth, github-app-token</td><td><code>GET api.github.com/user</code></td><td>200 is live; 401/403 is inactive.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>gitlab-pat, gitlab-pipeline-token</td><td><code>GET gitlab.com/api/v4/user</code></td><td>Uses <code>Private-Token</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>stripe-live-secret, stripe-test-secret, stripe-live-restricted</td><td><code>GET api.stripe.com/v1/balance</code></td><td>An accepted test credential is still labelled live; <code>livemode</code> is only detail.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>slack-bot-token, slack-user-token</td><td><code>POST slack.com/api/auth.test</code></td><td>HTTP 200 with <code>ok: false</code> becomes inactive.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>openai-api-key</td><td><code>GET api.openai.com/v1/models</code></td><td>Requires access to that endpoint.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>anthropic-api-key</td><td><code>GET api.anthropic.com/v1/models</code></td><td>Sends <code>x-api-key</code> and an API-version header.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>sendgrid-api-key</td><td><code>GET api.sendgrid.com/v3/user/profile</code></td><td>Scope or endpoint policy can affect the result.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>huggingface-token</td><td><code>GET huggingface.co/api/whoami-v2</code></td><td>Scope or endpoint policy can affect the result.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>aws-access-key-id</td><td>No request</td><td>An access-key ID alone cannot sign STS; the adapter returns unknown.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>gcp-api-key</td><td><code>GET oauth2.googleapis.com/tokeninfo?access_token=…</code></td><td>The rule name says API key while the endpoint validates OAuth access tokens; interpret this adapter cautiously.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>twilio-api-key</td><td><code>GET api.twilio.com/2010-04-01/Accounts.json</code></td><td>Basic auth needs an appropriate SID/token pair; a lone detected value may be insufficient.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>supabase-key</td><td><code>GET https://&lt;ref&gt;.supabase.co/rest/v1/</code></td><td>The project ref must be decoded from the JWT payload.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>vercel-token</td><td><code>GET api.vercel.com/v2/user</code></td><td>200 is live; 401/403 is inactive.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>netlify-token</td><td><code>GET api.netlify.com/api/v1/user</code></td><td>200 is live; 401/403 is inactive.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>cloudflare-api-key, cloudflare-api-token</td><td><code>GET api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/user/tokens/verify</code></td><td>The response must also say the token is active.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>linear-api-key</td><td><code>POST api.linear.app/graphql</code></td><td>Runs a viewer query; a 200 without viewer data becomes inactive.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Only these exact rule IDs are routed to an adapter. Other detections return <code>unknown</code> without a provider request.</p>

<h2 id="timeouts">Timeouts and concurrency</h2>
<p>Each adapter is raced against a 2-second timeout. Findings are scheduled concurrently. The code also checks a nominal 5-second budget before starting each task, but it does not enforce a strict aggregate wall-clock deadline after all concurrent tasks begin. There are no retries or backoff in this layer.</p>

<h2 id="cache">Local cache</h2>
<p>Results are cached for one hour at <code>~/.gate/verify-cache.json</code> (or under <code>GATE_HOME</code>). The cache key contains the rule ID and a full SHA-256 hash of the credential, not the raw credential. Cached result details may include provider-returned account labels such as a login, username, Slack user, or team. Gate requests owner-only file mode on supported systems, but filesystem, backup, and sync behavior still applies.</p>
<p>A cache hit reuses the old result and reapplies the severity change without contacting the provider. Revocation, account changes, or provider-side updates during the TTL may therefore be hidden until the entry expires or the cache is removed.</p>

<h2 id="read-only">“Read-only” is an intent, not a guarantee</h2>
<p>The current adapters use identity, token-validation, model-list, balance-read, or similar requests and do not intentionally create or modify provider resources. Two adapters use POST for query-style operations. APIs can change, access logs may be written, billing or rate limits may apply, and Gate has not independently audited every provider's side effects.</p>

<h2 id="practical">Recommended use</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Run the ordinary local scan first.</li>
  <li>Review the exact adapter above before opting in.</li>
  <li>Use verification as one evidence field, never as permission to keep a credential in source.</li>
  <li>Rotate anything committed or pushed, even when Gate says inactive or unknown.</li>
  <li>Keep CI thresholds based on the original detection risk until the inactive downgrade behavior is changed.</li>
</ol>
